<Docs
  title="Getting started"
  description="Install MrTerminal on macOS, Windows or Linux, add a project, run its scripts in one click, arrange terminals, save presets and restore sessions."
>
  <h1>Getting started</h1>
  <p>
    MrTerminal is a desktop app that holds the terminals your projects need. It isn't a new shell and it isn't a
    terminal emulator: each terminal is your own login shell, drawn with xterm.js and arranged in splits and tabs.
  </p>

  <h2>Install</h2>
  <p>
    Download the <code>.dmg</code> from the <a href="https://github.com/kthilagarajan/mrterminal/releases/latest"
      >latest release</a
    >. Use <code>arm64</code> for Apple silicon and <code>x64</code> for Intel. Drag MrTerminal to Applications.
    The app isn't notarized by Apple yet, so macOS blocks the first launch. Clear the download flag once:
  </p>
  <pre><code>xattr -cr /Applications/MrTerminal.app</code></pre>
  <p>
    MrTerminal checks for updates when it starts and every few hours. When an update is ready, an <strong>Update to
    x.y.z</strong> button appears in the top bar.
  </p>

  <p>
    On Windows 10/11 (x64), download and run the <code>win-x64-setup.exe</code> installer. The app is unsigned.
    PowerShell is the default shell; PowerShell 7 can be selected in Settings.
    On Linux (x64), use the <code>.deb</code> package or make the <code>.AppImage</code> executable and run it.
    AppImages need FUSE 2, or use <code>--appimage-extract-and-run</code>.
    On Windows and Linux, the update button opens the release page for manual installation.
    After installing the CLI on Windows, add the displayed bin directory to your user Path and open a new terminal.
  </p>

  <h2>Add a project</h2>
  <p>
    Press <kbd>⌘O</kbd> or the <strong>+</strong> next to the logo and pick a repository. MrTerminal reads the
    scripts it defines (<code>package.json</code>, <code>Makefile</code>, <code>pyproject.toml</code>,
    <code>justfile</code>, <code>Procfile</code>, <code>compose.yaml</code>, <code>Cargo.toml</code>,
    <code>go.mod</code>, and the same files in sub-folders) and lists them on the project's card.
  </p>

  <h2>Run, stop, arrange</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A green ▶ starts a script in its own terminal. A red ■ stops it, as Ctrl-C would.</li>
    <li>Running a script that already has a terminal shows that terminal instead of opening another one.</li>
    <li>A project card lists what is running in it right now, even when the card is folded.</li>
    <li>Drag tabs to split, stack and resize. Each project keeps its own layout.</li>
    <li>Add commands that aren't in any script file under <strong>Custom commands</strong>.</li>
    <li><kbd>⇧⌘T</kbd> reopens the tab or project you just closed.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Presets and sessions</h2>
  <p>
    A preset is a set of terminals and their layout that opens in one click. Arrange what a task needs, then press
    <strong>+</strong> next to Presets. When you quit and reopen, MrTerminal asks whether to resume your terminals
    and run their commands again, restore only the layout, or start fresh. You can set a default in Settings.
  </p>

  <h2>From the command line</h2>
  <p>
    Choose <strong>Shell → Install 'mrt' Command…</strong> to control MrTerminal from any shell, or let your coding
    agent do it. See the <a href={`${base}docs/cli/`}>command reference</a> and
    <a href={`${base}docs/agents/`}>coding agents</a>.
  </p>
</Docs>
